(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-12-03"] = {"mth-12-03-m01":{"idea":"Sine is zero at every multiple of π.","steps":["sin θ = 0 at 0, π, 2π, −π and so on.","All of these are multiples of π.","So θ = nπ, where n is any integer."],"others":"2nπ gives only every second zero. (2n + 1)π/2 is where cosine is zero. nπ/2 includes points where sine is not zero.","trap":"Sine zero: nπ. Cosine zero: (2n + 1)π/2.","answer":"θ = nπ, where n is any integer"},"mth-12-03-m02":{"idea":"Cosine is zero at the odd multiples of π/2.","steps":["cos θ = 0 at π/2, 3π/2, 5π/2, −π/2 and so on.","These are the odd multiples of π/2.","So θ = (2n + 1)π/2."],"others":"2nπ is where cosine is 1. (2n + 1)π is where cosine is −1. nπ is where sine is zero.","trap":"Odd multiples of π/2.","answer":"θ = (2n + 1)π/2, where n is any integer"},"mth-12-03-m03":{"idea":"Sine is positive in the first two quadrants.","steps":["sin θ = 1/2, so the reference angle is π/6.","First quadrant: π/6.","Second quadrant: π − π/6 = 5π/6."],"others":"π/3 and 2π/3 are for sin θ = √3/2. π/6 and 11π/6 would need sine to be positive and negative. π/6 and 7π/6 are where sine is 1/2 and −1/2.","trap":"Second-quadrant angle = π − α.","answer":"π/6 and 5π/6"},"mth-12-03-m04":{"idea":"For cos θ = cos α the general solution has a plus-or-minus sign.","steps":["cos(π/3) = 1/2, so α = π/3.","cos θ = cos α gives θ = 2nπ ± α.","So θ = 2nπ ± π/3."],"others":"nπ + (−1)ⁿ π/3 is the sine form. 2nπ ± π/6 uses the wrong angle. nπ ± π/3 uses the wrong period.","trap":"Cosine: 2nπ ± α.","answer":"θ = 2nπ ± π/3"},"mth-12-03-m05":{"idea":"Tangent repeats every π, so its solution has no plus-or-minus.","steps":["tan(π/3) = √3, so α = π/3.","tan θ = tan α gives θ = nπ + α.","So θ = nπ + π/3."],"others":"nπ + π/6 uses the wrong angle. 2nπ + π/3 misses the repeats every π. nπ ± π/3 adds a sign that tangent does not need.","trap":"Tangent: nπ + α.","answer":"θ = nπ + π/3"},"mth-12-03-m06":{"idea":"Sine takes the same value at α and π − α, which the formula covers.","steps":["For even n: θ = nπ + α.","For odd n: θ = nπ − α.","So θ = nπ + (−1)ⁿ α covers both α and π − α and their repeats."],"others":"nπ + α misses the second family. nπ ± α is the tangent-squared type. 2nπ ± α belongs to cosine.","trap":"Sine: nπ + (−1)ⁿ α.","answer":"θ = nπ + (−1)ⁿ α"},"mth-12-03-m07":{"idea":"Squares give the plus-or-minus form with period π.","steps":["sin²(π/6) = 1/4, so α = π/6.","sin²θ = sin²α gives θ = nπ ± α.","So θ = nπ ± π/6 (this covers sin θ = 1/2 and −1/2 together)."],"others":"nπ + (−1)ⁿ π/3 and nπ ± π/3 use the wrong angle. 2nπ ± π/6 misses half of the solutions.","trap":"For squares: nπ ± α.","answer":"θ = nπ ± π/6"},"mth-12-03-m08":{"idea":"Factorise the quadratic in sin θ and count the angles in [0, 2π].","steps":["2sin²θ − 3 sin θ + 1 = (2 sin θ − 1)(sin θ − 1) = 0.","sin θ = 1/2 gives π/6 and 5π/6.","sin θ = 1 gives π/2 only.","In all there are 3 solutions."],"others":"1 and 2 miss some roots. 4 counts the repeated π/2 twice.","trap":"sin θ = 1 gives only one angle.","answer":"3"},"mth-12-03-m09":{"idea":"Never divide by sin θ without checking that it can be zero.","steps":["Write sin θ (cos θ − 1) = 0.","Either sin θ = 0, giving θ = nπ.","Or cos θ = 1, giving θ = 2nπ, which is already inside nπ.","So the solution is θ = nπ."],"others":"Cancelling sin θ loses the solutions where sin θ = 0. (2n + 1)π/2 is where cosine is zero. nπ/2 includes points that are not solutions.","trap":"Cancelling a factor can lose solutions.","answer":"θ = nπ, since sin θ = 0 also gives solutions"},"mth-12-03-m10":{"idea":"The sine rule links each side with the sine of the opposite angle.","steps":["Each side divided by the sine of its opposite angle gives the same number.","That number is 2R, where R is the circumradius.","So a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C."],"others":"The forms with cos, with products a sin A, and with mismatched sides and angles are not the sine rule.","trap":"Side over sine of the opposite angle.","answer":"a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C"},"mth-12-03-m11":{"idea":"The cosine rule can be rearranged to find an angle.","steps":["a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A.","Rearrange: 2bc cos A = b² + c² − a².","cos A = (b² + c² − a²)/(2bc)."],"others":"(a² + b² − c²)/(2ab) is cos C. Dropping the 2 or adding a² gives wrong formulas.","trap":"The angle's own side goes with the minus sign.","answer":"(b² + c² − a²)/(2bc)"},"mth-12-03-m12":{"idea":"Heron's formula needs the semi-perimeter.","steps":["s = (a + b + c)/2.","Area = √(s(s − a)(s − b)(s − c)).","No extra factor of 1/2 is needed."],"others":"Without the square root the units would be wrong. Leaving out s is incorrect. A factor 1/2 would halve the area.","trap":"Square root of the whole product.","answer":"√(s(s − a)(s − b)(s − c))"},"mth-12-03-m13":{"idea":"A side is the sum of the projections of the other two sides onto it.","steps":["Project side b onto side a: b cos C.","Project side c onto side a: c cos B.","So a = b cos C + c cos B."],"others":"a = c cos C + b cos B uses the wrong angles. The sine form and the form with cos B and cos C of their own sides are also wrong.","trap":"Use the angle next to side a.","answer":"a = b cos C + c cos B"},"mth-12-03-m14":{"idea":"The largest angle is opposite the longest side.","steps":["The longest side is 5, so the largest angle C is opposite it.","cos C = (3² + 4² − 5²)/(2 × 3 × 4) = 0.","So C = 90°. (3² + 4² = 5², so it is a right-angled triangle.)"],"others":"45°, 60° and 120° do not match cos C = 0.","trap":"3-4-5 is a right triangle.","answer":"90°"},"mth-12-03-m15":{"idea":"Use the sine rule to find a side.","steps":["b = a sin B/sin A.","sin 45° = √2/2 and sin 30° = 1/2.","b = 2 × (√2/2)/(1/2) = 2√2."],"others":"√2, 2√3 and 4 come from using the wrong angles in the ratio.","trap":"Put the unknown side's angle on top.","answer":"2√2"},"mth-12-03-m16":{"idea":"The half-angle cosine uses s and s − a on top.","steps":["cos(A/2) = √(s(s − a)/(bc)).","Sine uses (s − b)(s − c) on top instead.","Both have bc below and are under a square root."],"others":"√((s − b)(s − c)/(bc)) is for sin(A/2). The version with ab below has the wrong sides. The one without a square root is wrong.","trap":"Cosine: s(s − a).","answer":"√(s(s − a)/(bc))"},"mth-12-03-m17":{"idea":"The principal branch of sin⁻¹ is the interval where sine is one-one.","steps":["Sine is one-one on [−π/2, π/2].","It takes all values from −1 to 1 there.","So the range of sin⁻¹ x is [−π/2, π/2]."],"others":"The open interval leaves out the end values. [−π, π] is not one-one. [0, π] is the range of cos⁻¹.","trap":"sin⁻¹: [−π/2, π/2]. cos⁻¹: [0, π].","answer":"[−π/2, π/2]"},"mth-12-03-m18":{"idea":"The principal branch of cos⁻¹ is [0, π].","steps":["Cosine is one-one on [0, π].","It takes all values from 1 down to −1 there.","So cos⁻¹ x lies between 0 and π."],"others":"[−π/2, π/2] is for sin⁻¹. (0, π) leaves out the ends. [0, 2π] is not one-one.","trap":"cos⁻¹ never gives a negative angle.","answer":"[0, π]"},"mth-12-03-m19":{"idea":"sin⁻¹(sin x) gives x only when x is in [−π/2, π/2].","steps":["sin(5π/6) = 1/2.","sin⁻¹(1/2) must lie in [−π/2, π/2], so it is π/6.","5π/6 is outside the principal range."],"others":"−π/6 has the wrong sign. 5π/6 is not in the range. π/3 has the wrong sine.","trap":"The answer must lie in the principal range.","answer":"π/6"},"mth-12-03-m20":{"idea":"The two inverse functions give complementary angles.","steps":["sin⁻¹ x + cos⁻¹ x = π/2 for −1 ≤ x ≤ 1.","Example: x = 1/2 gives π/6 + π/3 = π/2.","So the answer is always π/2."],"others":"π, 0 and π/4 do not match the examples.","trap":"A fixed identity, not dependent on x.","answer":"π/2"},"mth-12-03-m21":{"idea":"cos⁻¹(−x) = π − cos⁻¹ x.","steps":["cos⁻¹(1/2) = π/3.","cos⁻¹(−1/2) = π − π/3 = 2π/3.","That lies in [0, π]."],"others":"−π/3 is negative, which cos⁻¹ never gives. 5π/6 is the angle for −√3/2. π/3 is the angle for +1/2.","trap":"A negative argument gives an angle above π/2.","answer":"2π/3"},"mth-12-03-m22":{"idea":"When the product of the two tangents is more than 1, add π.","steps":["2 × 3 = 6, which is greater than 1.","tan⁻¹ 2 + tan⁻¹ 3 = π + tan⁻¹((2 + 3)/(1 − 6)).","= π + tan⁻¹(−1) = π − π/4 = 3π/4."],"others":"π/2 would need the product to be exactly 1. −π/4 forgets to add π. π/4 is the answer only when the product is less than 1 and the sum 1.","trap":"xy > 1 means add π.","answer":"3π/4"},"mth-12-03-m23":{"idea":"When the product is less than 1, use the plain sum formula.","steps":["Product = 1/2 × 1/3 = 1/6, which is less than 1.","Sum = tan⁻¹((1/2 + 1/3)/(1 − 1/6)).","= tan⁻¹((5/6)/(5/6)) = tan⁻¹ 1 = π/4."],"others":"tan⁻¹(5/6) forgets to divide by 1 − xy. π/6 and π/2 do not match.","trap":"Remember the denominator 1 − xy.","answer":"π/4"},"mth-12-03-m24":{"idea":"Draw a right triangle from the angle.","steps":["Let θ = cos⁻¹(3/5), so cos θ = 3/5 in the first quadrant.","sin θ = √(1 − 9/25) = √(16/25).","sin θ = 4/5 (positive, since θ is in [0, π/2])."],"others":"5/4 is more than 1, which is impossible. 3/5 is the cosine. 3/4 is the tangent.","trap":"3-4-5 triangle.","answer":"4/5"},"mth-12-03-m25":{"idea":"Use cos 2t in terms of tan t.","steps":["Let t = tan⁻¹(1/2), so tan t = 1/2.","cos 2t = (1 − tan²t)/(1 + tan²t).","= (1 − 1/4)/(1 + 1/4) = (3/4)/(5/4) = 3/5."],"others":"4/5 is sin 2t for this angle. 3/4 and 1/2 do not follow from the formula.","trap":"cos 2t = (1 − tan²t)/(1 + tan²t).","answer":"3/5"},"mth-12-03-m26":{"idea":"The −1 on a function name means the inverse function.","steps":["tan⁻¹ x is the inverse of tan.","Its values lie in (−π/2, π/2).","The reciprocal of tan x is cot x, written (tan x)⁻¹."],"others":"The reciprocal 1/tan x is cot x. 'tan x to the power −1' is a different notation. cot⁻¹ x is the inverse of cot, with range [0, π].","trap":"tan⁻¹ is not 1/tan.","answer":"the inverse function of tan, with values in (−π/2, π/2)"},"mth-12-03-m27":{"idea":"Inverse sine is an odd function.","steps":["Sine is odd: sin(−y) = −sin y.","So its inverse is odd too.","sin⁻¹(−x) = −sin⁻¹ x."],"others":"sin⁻¹ x would mean it is even. π/2 − sin⁻¹ x is cos⁻¹ x. π − sin⁻¹ x is not a valid identity.","trap":"cos⁻¹(−x) = π − cos⁻¹ x is the cosine rule.","answer":"−sin⁻¹ x"},"mth-12-03-n01":{"idea":"Solve cos θ = 1/2 and count the angles in [0, 4π].","steps":["2 cos θ = 1 gives cos θ = 1/2.","General solution: θ = 2nπ ± π/3.","In [0, 4π]: π/3, 5π/3, 7π/3 and 11π/3.","That is 4 solutions."],"trap":"[0, 4π] holds two full turns.","answer":"4"},"mth-12-03-n02":{"idea":"Tangent repeats every π, so find the solutions and add them.","steps":["tan θ = 1 gives θ = nπ + π/4.","In [0, 2π]: π/4 and 5π/4.","The sum is 6π/4 = 3π/2 = 4.712."],"trap":"Two solutions only in one full turn.","answer":"4.712"},"mth-12-03-n03":{"idea":"A square equation gives both signs.","steps":["4 sin²θ − 1 = 0 gives sin²θ = 1/4, so sin θ = 1/2 or −1/2.","sin θ = 1/2: π/6 and 5π/6.","sin θ = −1/2: 7π/6 and 11π/6.","That is 4 solutions."],"trap":"Do not forget the negative root.","answer":"4"},"mth-12-03-n04":{"idea":"The largest angle is opposite the longest side. Use the cosine rule.","steps":["The longest side is 13.","cos C = (7² + 8² − 13²)/(2 × 7 × 8) = (49 + 64 − 169)/112.","= −56/112 = −1/2, so C = 120°."],"trap":"A negative cosine means an obtuse angle.","answer":"120°"},"mth-12-03-n05":{"idea":"Use Heron's formula.","steps":["s = (13 + 14 + 15)/2 = 21.","Area = √(21 × 8 × 7 × 6) = √7056 = 84."],"trap":"s − a = 8, s − b = 7, s − c = 6.","answer":"84"},"mth-12-03-n06":{"idea":"The circumradius is R = abc/(4 × area).","steps":["abc = 13 × 14 × 15 = 2730.","4 × area = 4 × 84 = 336.","R = 2730/336 = 8.125."],"trap":"Check: the sine rule form a/(2 sin of the opposite angle) gives the same value.","answer":"8.125"},"mth-12-03-n07":{"idea":"Add the two inverse tangents with the tan sum formula.","steps":["The product 1/6 is less than 1.","Sum = tan⁻¹((1/2 + 1/3)/(1 − 1/6)) = tan⁻¹((5/6)/(5/6)).","= tan⁻¹ 1 = 45°."],"trap":"No π needs to be added here.","answer":"45°"},"mth-12-03-n08":{"idea":"When the product is more than 1, add π.","steps":["The product 2 × 3 = 6 is greater than 1.","tan⁻¹ 2 + tan⁻¹ 3 = π + tan⁻¹(5/(−5)) = π − π/4.","= 3π/4 = 2.356 radians."],"trap":"3π/4 = 135°.","answer":"2.356"},"mth-12-03-n09":{"idea":"Use cos 2t = (1 − tan²t)/(1 + tan²t).","steps":["tan t = 1/2, so tan²t = 1/4.","cos 2t = (1 − 1/4)/(1 + 1/4) = (3/4)/(5/4).","= 3/5 = 0.600."],"trap":"No need to find the angle itself.","answer":"0.600"},"mth-12-03-n10":{"idea":"Find the sine and cosine of each angle, then use the sum formula.","steps":["Let the first angle have sin = 3/5, so cos = 4/5.","Let the second angle have cos = 5/13, so sin = 12/13.","sin of the sum = (3/5)(5/13) + (4/5)(12/13) = 15/65 + 48/65.","= 63/65 = 0.9692."],"trap":"Use sin(x + y) = sin x cos y + cos x sin y.","answer":"0.9692"},"mth-12-03-n11":{"idea":"2 radians is outside [−π/2, π/2], so use π − 2.","steps":["2 radians is in the second quadrant (π/2 is about 1.571).","sin 2 = sin(π − 2), and π − 2 = 1.142 is inside the principal range.","So sin⁻¹(sin 2) = π − 2 = 1.142."],"trap":"The answer is not 2.","answer":"1.142"}};
